{"prdMarkdown":"# PRD: Edit & Resubmit Clarification Answers\n\n## Problem\nCurrently, users who review generated PRD and TRD documents have no way to iterate on their responses if they realize an answer was incomplete, inaccurate, or needs refinement based on the generated findings. Users are forced to restart the entire workflow in a brand new session, re-entering their initial feature prompt, waiting for clarification questions to re-generate, and filling out all questions again from scratch.\n\n## Goals\n- Allow users to review their previously submitted answers alongside the generated PRD/TRD in `public/index.html`.\n- Enable inline editing of existing answers and resubmission within the same session.\n- Automatically re-run downstream pipeline stages (blast radius heuristic, edge cases, question classification, estimate, and document generation) to reflect updated answers.\n- Restrict editing and resubmission strictly to sessions that have not yet been exported to GitHub via `src/pipeline/githubWriteback.js`.\n\n## Non-goals\n- Document and finding version history / audit logs: old artifacts will be cleanly overwritten in place for simplicity.\n- Re-generating new clarifying questions via `src/pipeline/clarify.js`: the clarification questions remain stable; only user answers change.\n- Modifying sessions already pushed to GitHub (exporting creates a finalized external artifact).\n- Branching or diff-comparison interfaces between pipeline runs in this initial release.\n\n## User impact\n- **Product Managers & Developers:** Greatly reduced friction when refining specs. If a generated estimate or edge-case finding highlights an unforeseen complication, users can quickly revise their answers to scope down or clarify assumptions without losing their workflow context.\n- **First-Time & Iterative Users:** Prevents excise and frustration caused by re-entering the original feature ask and re-answering unchanged questions.\n\n## Open product questions\n- Should users be permitted to modify the original raw feature request prompt text during resubmission, or strictly edit their answers to the existing clarifying questions?\n- For sessions already exported to GitHub, should the UI simply disable/hide the edit controls, or offer a 'duplicate as new session' action to allow further refinement?","trdMarkdown":"# TRD: Edit & Resubmit Clarification Answers\n\n## Architecture notes\n- **Route & Validation Layer (`src/routes/sessions.js`):**\n  - Implement or extend an endpoint (e.g., `PUT` or `POST /api/sessions/:id/answers` or `/resubmit`) accepting revised answers.\n  - Validate that the session exists and has not completed a GitHub write-back (`github_issue_url` or similar export marker in `src/db/schema.sql`). If already exported, return `409 Conflict` or `400 Bad Request`.\n  - Orchestrate database mutation and pipeline re-triggering.\n- **Pipeline Orchestration (`src/pipeline/index.js`):**\n  - Differentiate between initial session generation and answer resubmission. \n  - During resubmission, explicitly bypass Step 2 (`src/pipeline/clarify.js`) to avoid generating mismatched questions. Pass updated answers into downstream stages: `src/pipeline/blastRadius.js`, `src/pipeline/edgeCases.js`, `src/pipeline/classifier.js`, `src/pipeline/estimate.js`, `src/pipeline/docGenerator.js`, and `src/pipeline/coverageGate.js`.\n- **Database Storage & Mutation (`src/db/schema.sql`, `src/db/index.js`):**\n  - Overwrite existing findings, estimates, and PRD/TRD markdown in place within a database transaction to prevent orphaned records or foreign key constraint issues.\n  - Update stored session answers with the revised payload.\n- **UI Layer (`public/index.html`):**\n  - Populate the answer fields with existing session values when transitioning to or viewing the results view.\n  - Provide an \"Edit Answers\" / \"Resubmit\" action that re-activates the form controls.\n  - If the session state indicates successful GitHub write-back, disable or conceal the resubmit action.\n  - Maintain loading and progress indicators during the pipeline re-run.\n\n## Edge cases & dependencies\n- **GitHub Write-back Lock:** Must be enforced at both the API (`src/routes/sessions.js`) and UI (`public/index.html`) levels to prevent desynchronization between GitHub issues and local session state.\n- **Clarification Question Mismatch:** If Step 2 (`src/pipeline/clarify.js`) runs again, it produces non-deterministic questions that will not correspond to the user's updated answer keys. Bypassing Step 2 during resubmissions is required.\n- **Database Constraints & Cascades:** In `src/db/schema.sql`, overwriting or deleting old findings and docs before inserting new ones must handle cascading deletes properly to prevent foreign key errors.\n- **Concurrent Resubmissions:** Double-clicking the resubmit button or concurrent API calls could trigger overlapping LLM and database transactions. A session status check or transactional lock is required.\n- **Request Latency / Timeouts:** Executing edge cases, classification, and doc generation involves multiple sequential LLM calls (`src/llm/index.js`), risking HTTP timeout if handled synchronously.\n\n## Engineering questions\n- Should pipeline re-execution explicitly bypass Step 2 (`src/pipeline/clarify.js`) and run stages 3 through 8 with updated answers to avoid re-generating mismatched clarifying questions?\n- Should the resubmission endpoint process the multi-LLM pipeline synchronously or adopt an asynchronous job/status polling mechanism to prevent HTTP timeouts?\n- How should concurrent resubmissions or duplicate requests for an active session be guarded against (e.g., in-flight session status lock in Postgres via `src/db/index.js` vs. client-side debounce in `public/index.html`)?\n\n## Estimate\n- **Estimate Range:** 2-4 days (up to 9 days worst case if async polling and state locking require architectural refactoring)."}